	"identifying_description": "Leaf of paper with etching and hand colored with opaque watercolor, showing river scene with a woman riding a mule, a mule goaded by a man with a stick and pulled by another man at front, two men in a boat, two seated women and two standing men. Plain reverse. Carved wood and ivory sticks and guards. Ivory thumb guard. Metal rivet.",
	"web_text": "Fans with printed leaves were first produced in the 1720s in England and quickly became popular with women across all social classes. Printed leaves could be reproduced more quickly and less expensively than those with painted designs. Printed fans were often used to circulate political news; they were widely produced in France during the French Revolution to show political sympathies and to commemorate important events. The clothing worn by the women on right of this fan, although not overtly political, shows the shift in fashion during the revolutionary period to simple styles modeled after classical modes.",
	"didactic_label": "Fans with printed leaves were first produced in the 1720s in England and quickly became popular with women across all social classes. Printed leaves could be reproduced more quickly and less expensively than those with painted designs. Printed fans were often used to circulate political news; they were widely produced in France during the French Revolution to show political sympathies and to commemorate important events. The clothing worn by the women on right of this fan, although not overtly political, shows the shift in fashion during the revolutionary period to simple styles modeled after classical modes.",
	"exhibition_history_text": "Fans of the Eighteenth Century, Textiles Education Gallery, de Young Museum, 3/31/18 - 11/2/18",
